\description{
Decomposes the smoothed factor path, and the nowcast of the fit's \code{target},
into the contribution of each input series (or of each group of series) in
every period. Answers "which series drive the factor, and by how much, when?"
}
\details{
The decomposition is the smoother's own. Conditional on the posterior mean
parameters and volatility path, the posterior mean of the factor path is a
linear function of the observed data, \verb{E[f | y, theta] = W y}, and summing the
columns of \code{W} that belong to one series gives that series' contribution. It
is exact: the contributions sum to \code{E[f | y, theta]} to numerical tolerance,
a series whose loading is zero contributes zero, and an observation carries
weight in a period even when the series was not observed in it -- which is
the whole point, and is why "loading times standardised series" is not an
answer to this question. That product is the series' reconstruction \emph{from}
the factor and does not sum to anything.

Everything is reported on the \strong{standardised, non-annualised scale the
sampler works on}, which is the scale on which the decomposition is linear.
\verb{$factor} on an \code{\link[=ind_dfm]{ind_dfm()}} fit is de-standardised and annualised through
\code{((1 + f)^frequency - 1) * 100}; that transformation is not linear, so
contributions to it do not exist. \code{totals$posterior} maps the fit's own
posterior mean back to the sampler's scale so that the two are comparable.
\subsection{The residual term}{

\code{E[f | y, theta-hat]} is not the same thing as the MCMC posterior mean factor,
which averages over the posterior of \code{theta} rather than plugging in its mean.
The difference is reported rather than hidden: \code{totals} carries \code{plugin} (the
sum of the contributions), \code{posterior} (the fit's own posterior mean, mapped
to the same scale) and \code{residual = posterior - plugin}. Read \code{residual} as
parameter uncertainty. It shrinks as the chain lengthens and as the sample
grows; on a short chain over a couple of years it is not small, and a
decomposition whose residual dominates should not be read as if it explained
the factor.
}

\subsection{Contributions to the nowcast}{

The target's value in period \code{i} is the aggregated factor plus its own
measurement error,
\verb{x[i] = sum(L[sx] * lambda' f[i - sx]) + u[i]}, so the factor contributions
are carried through the target's loading and the model's own distributed-lag
aggregation weights \code{L}. \code{nowcast_totals} splits the nowcast into
\code{systematic} (the sum of those contributions) and \code{idiosyncratic}
(\code{E[x | y, theta-hat]} minus the systematic part, i.e. the measurement error
the smoother attributes to the target itself). Where the target was actually
observed, \code{E[x | y]} is the observation, and the idiosyncratic term absorbs
whatever the factor does not explain.
}

\subsection{Both classes}{

\code{\link[=fcast_dfm]{fcast_dfm()}} gets one decomposition per factor. No extra rotation is needed:
\code{run_rotation_fcast()} and \code{run_identification_fcast()} rotate the draws
themselves, so the stored posterior mean \code{lambda} and \code{phi} are already in the
identified orientation and \code{W} is built from them.

The reported time axis covers all \code{t+s} periods of the factor path, including
the \code{s} pre-sample periods the distributed lags carry. \code{totals$posterior} is
read from \verb{$factor} for a \code{\link[=fcast_dfm]{fcast_dfm()}} fit and from \verb{$factor_std} for an
\code{\link[=ind_dfm]{ind_dfm()}} fit, both on the sampler's scale over all \code{t+s} periods. (An
\code{\link[=ind_dfm]{ind_dfm()}} fit saved before \verb{$factor_std} existed falls back to inverting
the annualised \verb{$factor}, which leaves the pre-sample periods \code{NA}.)
}
